  abbrev_source_title    = {Tasmimgiri. Tahqiq. Amaliyyat.},
  abstract               = {Purpose: The purpose of this paper is to respond to the constant challenge of predicting prices of food commodities in developing economies, which are highly volatile, affecting food security and making policy decisions more difficult. The goal of this study is to develop an end-to-end framework that is reliable, scalable, and complementary to supporting both policy and market interventions in real time. Methodology: The paper tests a wide range of Machine Learning (ML), Deep Learning (DL), hybrid, and statistical models on a large-scale dataset of 22 commodities across seven states in India. A common benchmarking framework is used to measure performance, and Friedman and Wilcoxon tests are used to determine statistical significance. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP)-based explainability is applied to determine important predictive factors. Findings: Random Forest (RF) was found to have the best forecasting performance (Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) = 1.49; R<sup>2</sup> = 0.9995), followed by a hybrid Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU)-XGBoost residual model. Classical methods such as SARIMA and Prophet lagged badly. Statistical tests confirmed that tree-based and hybrid models outperform DL and econometric baselines. SHAP analysis revealed that recent lags, rolling means, and volatility measures were the most influential predictors. Originality/Value: This work provides a comprehensive comparison of forecasting techniques for food commodities in India, demonstrating the practical value of ensemble and hybrid models. The study illuminates their efficacy, transparency, and suitability for real-time deployment for policymakers and supply chain stakeholders. © 2026, Research Expansion Alliance (REA). All rights reserved.},
